
Sub: Minutes of your RT Conference.

>From Steno: Jennifer Marlowe

Date: 02/19/89   Time: 21:15EST

Attendees:[[Jax] FIGCHAPTERS]
          [[Dennis] D.RUFFER]
          [[Dave] DHWEINSTEIN]
          [[Kevin] APPERT]
          [R.BERKEY]

Minutes:

<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> ok, I think that did it <grin>  now, Dave, would you
          repeat what you said about the conf for the record?

<[Dave - Conf] DHWEINSTEIN> The conference was a great success. The
          papers were interesting and well presented.  The proceedings
          will be a worthy inclusion in any Forth library.  (And,
          speaking for the record) Thanks to all who attended and to all
          those who wrote papers, whether or not they were able to
          attend.

<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> can you give us a quick run down on what was
          presented?  Will the proceedings be in electronic Form???
          <hoping>

<[Dave - Conf] DHWEINSTEIN> Papers included finite state automata,
          mutltitaskers (many and elegant), error handling, interrupts
          and interrupt handling, virtual memory without support
          hardware, local words,  Forth applications, and Forth control
          methods.

<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> How soon do you anticipate getting the proceedings
          out?

<[Dave - Conf] DHWEINSTEIN> The proceedings will be published by the ACM
          in 3 or 4 months (hopefully).  And of course, the computer
          with 17 megs of PD software to copy from was a ncie idea.

<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> Let FIG know, and I'm sure they will carry it (if
          that is allowed)

<[Dave - Conf] DHWEINSTEIN> (I have a whole bunch of goodies on disk
          now)  It will be sold by the ACM itself.

<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> I thought that was the case :-(

<[Dave - Conf] DHWEINSTEIN> And it looks like next year's conference
          will be in the Dallas-Ft. Worth area.

<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> 17Megs on whose computer?

<[Dave - Conf] DHWEINSTEIN> Dan brought his computer, and Warren Bean
          brought the programs.  Everything from the original F83 to
          FPC2.25  And everything in between. Plus things like Fifth,
          Pistol, Sixth, and even (surprise) SmallTalk.

<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> anything new that we don't have here?

<[Dave - Conf] DHWEINSTEIN> Possibly. I don't think GEnie has F-PC 2.25
          yet. I haven't even unarced it (it looks bigger then F-PC 2.15
          which I am using now).

<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> could you maybe upload it when you get a chance?

<[Dave - Conf] DHWEINSTEIN> I'll try.

<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> great!  Ok, what else we want to talk about tonight?

<[Dave - Conf] DHWEINSTEIN> Hmmn. I dunno.

<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> it's supposed to be a tutorial, is there anything I
          know that you guys don't <grin> (I doubt it).

<APPERT> is here.

<[Jax] FIGCHAPTERS> Talk about Tuesday Board Meeting.

<[Dave@Home] DHWEINSTEIN> Hi Kevin.

<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> again Jack, tuesday is NOT a board meeting

<[Kevin] APPERT> hi Dave, all

<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> Hi Kevin

<[Kevin] APPERT> hello Dennis  Jax making trouble again? :)

<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> Jack the Tuseday before the 4th Saturday of every
          month is the FIG BUsiness group meetings.   There they take
          care of the "real" business end of FIG

<[Jax] FIGCHAPTERS> Woof. Ok. WHen do i get to chat with board
          electronically.

<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> whenever we have the retreat.  Date has not been set
          yet :-(

<[Jax] FIGCHAPTERS> Everyone on my international mail list is waiting
          for some news!

<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> I wish I had some new to tell them  But we are all
          sort of hanging in limbo right now

<[Dave] DHWEINSTEIN> The JFAR's are printed and out! The Rochester
          Proceedings are done! Alles is gut!

<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> You were right when you said we had no direction
          right now  JFAR IS OUT???? what a surprise :-)

<[Dave] DHWEINSTEIN> Yep.

<[Jax] FIGCHAPTERS> I guess that's my news. "FIG is in Limbo"

<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> Was Larry there and handing them out Dave?

<[Dave] DHWEINSTEIN> Someone at the conf asked where Larry was. I said,
          "He'll be here when the JFAR get's here". Five minutes later
          he showed up with Box under arm.

<[Kevin] APPERT> anybody have any strong feelings about overflow
          handeling

<[Dave] DHWEINSTEIN> Yep. I have the Rochester proceedings in my hot
          little hands right now.

<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> you mean in math ops Kevin?

<[Kevin] APPERT> ?yup

<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> Larry had the Rochester proceedings at the FIG
          Convention  What do you want to do with the overflows Kevin?

<[Kevin] APPERT> not something Forth does much about, in my expeeience

<[Jax] FIGCHAPTERS> gnite all ... next stop sausalito ... dennis, let me
          know if FIG Board wants to chat ... Dave, talk to me on RCFB.
          gnite kev

<[Jax] FIGCHAPTERS> has left.

<[Kevin] APPERT> 'night Jax  oops, too slow

<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> me too

<[Dave] DHWEINSTEIN> Aren't you glad you're name is not Salman Rushdie?

<[Kevin] APPERT> as I was saying...

<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> what do you want to do with overflows Kevin?

<[Kevin] APPERT> who?

<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> the guy who wrote Satanic verses

<[Dave] DHWEINSTEIN> Salman Rushdie. Author of "The Satanic Verses"  Who
          has an 8 million dollar price on his head.

<[Kevin] APPERT> it's a big whole in the language, near as I can tell

<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> Mohamid is not too happy right now <grin>

<[Dave] DHWEINSTEIN> Time for me to move on as well.

<[Kevin] APPERT> oh, him.  The eye-at-hole-a  has sold a lot of books
          for him

<[Dave] DHWEINSTEIN> has left.

<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> bye Dave???

<[Kevin] APPERT> the two things you can do with overflows are setting a
          flag and an "abort" sort of thing...

<R.BERKEY> is here.

<[Kevin] APPERT> perhaps making note of where you came from which is not
          a bad thing... hi R.

<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> hi bob, we're talking about overflows

<[Kevin] APPERT> for any abort or interrupt to do  I just said you can
          set a flag or abort, I have not seen a whole lot of either
          done ever  16 bit arithm. is easy to overflow in many problems

<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> to do something about it Kevin, we would have to
          have an error mechanism, which we are lacking also

<[Kevin] APPERT> I disagree.  Abort has always been good  for me  some
          of my aborts know where they come from...

<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> it sounds like a design problem Kevin, if the number
          will overflow, you should use 32 bit ops

<[Kevin] APPERT> how do you know in advance???  everybody makes mistakes


<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> you "think" it through  or  when it fails you "fix
          it" <grin>

<[Kevin] APPERT> it's nice to have a little something to fall back on
          some of my code only has the opertunity to fail in low earth
          orbit

<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> there are a lot of "nice" things we could add to
          Forth, but would it still be forth when we got done?

<R.BERKEY> There are probably a lot of "nice" things we should be adding
          routinely,  but we still don't know about!

<[Kevin] APPERT> you are welcome to add whatever you want, that's the
          great part about forth

<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> there are those who would disagree Bob

<R.BERKEY> disagree?

<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> some people only "need" the minimal Forth

<[Kevin] APPERT> the other thing about aborts that I have seen, in
          addition to knowing where  they where when they bombed out...
          is one which returns there after it does some attempt at fix-
          up

<R.BERKEY> Kevin,  Colburn and Schleisiek wrote papers in the 1983 FORML
          about error trapping.

<[Kevin] APPERT> somebody gave a talk about that at silicone valley fig
          a year or so back  don't recall seeing it, R.  I'll have a
          look

<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> I've seen a lot of stuff done with errors, but
          little that I could use in an application.  Most of it was for
          the programmer, to give him hints about how to fix the
          problem.

<R.BERKEY> A manager once told me, get rid of all of the ABORT" s,
          unless you really  want a system programmer to be consulted.

<[Kevin] APPERT> most forth code is ment to be used by the guy who
          wrote it

<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> I disagree Kevin, most Forth code is embeded in
          turnkeyed applications where everything is custom and bears
          little resemblence to Forth once it is done.

<[Kevin] APPERT> It's hard to get out of that mindset into writing
          something ...  maintainable or usable by somebody you have
          never met.  oops dinners ready.  gnight all

<[Kevin] APPERT> has left.

<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> again too late

<R.BERKEY> It'swell, i think I shall follow

<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> it's been that way all night  thanks for droping by
          Bob

<R.BERKEY> I've left some messges for you in the standardss conference

<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> I'll give them a look, and probably reply (as usual
